## 33. What Linux issues can cause high CPU or memory usage?

**Answer:**

Common causes include:

* Infinite loops.
* Memory leaks.
* Heavy background jobs.
* Cron jobs running unexpectedly.
* Misconfigured applications.
* High traffic.
* Large or inefficient queries.
* Excessive logging.
* Too many application processes.
* Resource limits that are too low.

High resource usage can result in slow applications, timeouts, service crashes, and system instability.

---

## 34. What happens when Linux runs out of memory?

**Answer:**

When available RAM becomes low, Linux may use **swap space** if it is configured.

Swap is slower than RAM, so heavy swapping can significantly affect performance.

If memory pressure becomes severe, Linux may invoke the **OOM (Out of Memory) Killer**, which terminates selected processes to recover memory.

This can cause application downtime or unexpected restarts.

In production, I would investigate memory usage, application behavior, container limits, and system metrics to identify the root cause.

